#I recently concluded a targeted threat hunt investigating a multi-stage attack chain within a Windows development environment.
#By correlating Sysmon Event ID 1 (Process Creation) and Event ID 13 (Registry Value Set) telemetry inside Splunk,
#I traced the adversary's progression from an upstream package compromise to localized persistence.
#Here is the complete technical breakdown of the multi-stage attack timeline, mapped directly against the MITRE ATT&CK framework along with the hunt queries:

📦 STAGE 1: INITIAL ACCESS — Supply Chain Compromise (T1195.002)

* Tactical Focus: The adversary compromised an upstream software component to bypass traditional perimeter defenses, counting on the implicit trust built into developer workflows.
* Log Telemetry (Sysmon EID 1): Captured at 2025-06-21 10:58:24.000 on endpoint paw-tom under the high-integrity context of PAW-TOM\itadmin-tom.
* Technical Evidence & Artifacts: The internal build pipeline executed a legitimate runtime manager, node.exe (MD5: 0E35FC3B0EC975B76843501524C2F269), which pulled the malicious package dependency:
"C:\Program Files\nodejs\node.exe" "C:\Program Files\nodejs/node_modules/npm/bin/npm-cli.js" install healthchk-lib@1.0.1
* Splunk Hunt Query:

index=main EventCode=1 Image="*\\node.exe" CommandLine="*install healthchk-lib*"
| table _time, ComputerName, User, CommandLine, Hashes


💻 STAGE 2: EXECUTION — Command and Scripting Interpreter (T1059.001 / T1059.003)

* Tactical Focus: Exploiting a postinstall hook, the adversary generated a highly anomalous nested process tree to hide their execution environment.
* Log Telemetry (Sysmon EID 1): Captured at 10:58:27.000. The node.exe process (Guid: {c5d2b969-9050-6856-e101-000000002a01}) directly spawned cmd.exe (PID: 1616), which immediately cascaded into a stealthy PowerShell instance.
* Technical Evidence & Artifacts: The initial execution utilized a Unicode Base64-encoded command string:
cmd.exe /d /s /c powershell.exe -NoP -W Hidden -EncodedCommand JABkAGUAcwB0...
* Decoded Logic Analysis: De-obfuscation of the payload string revealed a multi-step script that declared a hidden path variable ($dest = "$env:APPDATA\SystemHealthUpdater.exe"), initiated a web pull using Invoke-WebRequest from a typosquatted domain (http://wlndows.thm), and spawned the downloaded binary.
* Splunk Hunt Query:

index=main EventCode=1 ParentImage="*\\node.exe" Image="*\\cmd.exe" CommandLine="*powershell.exe*EncodedCommand*"
| table _time, ComputerName, ParentCommandLine, CommandLine


🔑 STAGE 3: PERSISTENCE — Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Registry Run Keys (T1547.001)

* Tactical Focus: The secondary binary established an autostart hook within the target user's hive profile, ensuring persistent re-infection upon subsequent system interactive logons.
* Log Telemetry (Sysmon EID 13): Captured at 10:58:29.000. The script-driven powershell.exe instance (PID: 5880) executed a SetValue transaction to write the autostart rule.
* Technical Evidence & Artifacts: Rather than modifying global system keys, the transaction targeted a specific User SID profile hive:
HKU\S-1-5-21-1966530601-3185510712-10604624-500\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run\Windows Update Monitor
* Registry Value Payload: The entry was populated with an additional Base64-encoded command string that runs SystemHealthUpdater.exe straight out of the user's %APPDATA% Roaming folder every time the user logs in.
* Splunk Hunt Query:

index=main EventCode=13 TargetObject="*\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run\\Windows Update Monitor"
| table _time, ComputerName, Image, TargetObject, Details


------------------------------
🛡️ Engineering Detection & Blue Team Takeaways:

   1. Process Lineage Baselines: Build robust detection logic that triggers alerts when package management or build tools (npm, node, dotnet, pip) act as the parent process to generic command interpreters (cmd.exe, powershell.exe).
   2. User Hive Registry Baselines: Inspect SetValue actions targeting HKU:\*\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run paths when initiated by script hosts, especially if the payload runs out of writeable paths like %APPDATA% or %PUBLIC%.
   3. Pipeline Egress Restrictions: Implement zero-trust network boundaries on software build systems, restricting arbitrary runtime connections to external, unverified domains.

